Since the inception of grouping concept in K/Native in 2021 , there is a very similar set of heuristics there, whether to isolate test (a.k.a treat is as standalone), see [here](https://github.com/JetBrains/kotlin/blob/99487d8c6aa39a1eb740074b842c853a1652ab55/native/native.tests/testFixtures/org/jetbrains/kotlin/konan/test/blackbox/support/group/ExtTestCaseGroupProvider.kt#L270-L275)
Since 5 years it is proven as very conveninent DevEx for our developers, getting them free from guessing what to do (place a `NATIVE_STANDALONE` directive) after their new test is failed with random message

@ddolovov WDYT on this topic? - whether isolation heuristics are good thing, or WTF thing?

The current comment version is the following. Is it enough?
```
            // Dispatch on `isSingleTestBatch`, not `isIsolatedBatch`:
            //   a single-test batch must always be compiled as a standalone box-export test, since
            //   GenerateWasmTests.lower() never emits a grouped/unit-test-runner JUnit case for a single test,
            //   no matter it either was isolated or merely got a unique batch token.
            // `CustomWasmSecondStageFacade.Grouping` must use `isIsolatedBatch()`,
            //    since there `box()` is `@JsExport`-gated at first-stage compile time, before batch size is known.
            // But here, the in-process pipeline decides `box` export at the actual second-stage compilation (`wasmTestBoxFunctionToExport`),
            // so `isSingleTestBatch` is the correct, more precise criterion here.
```

